# Line endings — LF everywhere, no exceptions.
#
# This is not a style preference. Development happens on Windows while all the
# code runs on Linux: shell scripts, the Dockerfile, WSL configs. Git converts
# to CRLF in the working copy on Windows by default, and then `#!/bin/bash\r`
# on Linux produces "bad interpreter: No such file or directory" — the script
# does not run at all, and the error message gives no hint that a line ending
# is to blame.
#
# Anyone who cloned this repository on Windows and built the image would hit it:
# the build succeeds, the Box does not work.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows scripts are the EXCEPTION — they need CRLF.
# cmd.exe misbehaves unpredictably on LF-only files: multi-line `if (...)`
# blocks and `goto` labels get parsed wrongly. PowerShell is more forgiving, but
# we keep it uniform rather than guess.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# .ps1 must stay UTF-8 WITH BOM. Windows PowerShell 5.1 reads a BOM-less file as
# ANSI: non-ASCII characters fall apart, the parser dies, and the installer
# window closes instantly without a single message. That is exactly what
# happened on 2026-08-02. Git does not touch the BOM — this note is a warning,
# not a rule.

# Leave binaries alone.
*.png  binary
*.ico  binary
*.gguf binary
*.wsl  binary
*.zip  binary
